# The other half of tests/golden/micro/a_condition_is_asked_rather_than_read.
# When codegen fuses a comparison into its branch, the fast arm answers from
# the icmp and cannot fail, so the whole failure path belongs to the slow arm:
# the runtime compares, the answer is a failure, and the `if` answers it
# rather than choosing a side.
#
# The failure has to be BOUND INSIDE the body rather than handed in as an
# argument. A failing argument short-circuits the arm before its body runs, so
# a fixture written that way exits with the same message while never reaching
# the comparison at all — it was written that way first, and breaking the slow
# arm's failure test left it green.
#
# This is the condition in VALUE position, where the failure is one more arm
# of the `if`'s merge. Its twin, a_failing_condition_in_tail_position_returns,
# is the same question where the failure returns instead. The err is born in
# this hako so no arm here may match it, which is why the program exits
# non-zero and lives beside the runtime corpus rather than in micro.
fn asked n
  h = if (n == 0) (err "there is no number here") 5
  answer = if (h < 3) "lt" "ge"
  "chose {answer}"

pub play =
  print "{asked 1}"
  print "{asked 0}"
